 To implement the Sobel edge detection algorithm in MATLAB with a Gaussian filter, you can follow these steps:
1. Read the input image and convert it to grayscale.
2. Apply a Gaussian filter to reduce the noise in the image.
3. Define the Sobel operators in x and y directions.
4. Apply the Sobel operators in x and y directions using convolution after converting the image to double data type and padding with zeros to maintain the same size as the input image.
5. Combine the edges in both directions using the root sum square method.
6. Display the edge image.
Here's the modified code:
```
% Read input image and convert to grayscale
input_image = imread('image.jpg');
gray_image = rgb2gray(input_image);
% Define Gaussian filter (standard deviation of 1)
gaussian_filter = fspecial('gaussian', [3 3], 1);
% Apply Gaussian filter to reduce noise
filtered_image = imfilter(gray_image, gaussian_filter);
% Define Sobel operators in x and y directions
sobel_x = [-1 0 1; -2 0 2; -1 0 1];
sobel_y = [-1 -2 -1; 0 0 0; 1 2 1];
% Apply Sobel operator in x direction
edge_image_x = conv2(double(filtered_image), sobel_x, 'same');
% Apply Sobel operator in y direction
edge_image_y = conv2(double(filtered_image), sobel_y, 'same');
% Combine edges in both directions
edge_image = sqrt(edge_image_x.^2 + edge_image_y.^2);
% Display edge image
imshow(edge_image);
```
This code applies a Gaussian filter with a standard deviation of 1 to the input image before applying the Sobel operator to reduce the noise in the image. You can adjust the standard deviation value to suit your specific requirements. The `fspecial` function generates a predefined special filter, such as Gaussian, in MATLAB.

