 To make the circle element look more 3D, you can add some additional CSS properties such as a "transform" property to give it a perspective effect and a "transition" property for smooth animation. Here's an example:

```css
.circle {
  fill: radial-gradient(white, lightblue);
  filter: drop-shadow(15px 15px 15px gray);
  transition: all 0.3s ease-in-out;
  background: linear-gradient(to bottom, #fff, #ffcccc);
  box-shadow: 10px 10px 5px 0px rgba(0,0,0,0.75);
  border-radius: 70%;
  transform: translateZ(10px); // Add z-depth
}

.circle:hover {
  transform: translateZ(20px) rotateY(15deg); // Add rotation on hover
}
```

This will give your circle a subtle 3D appearance when hovered over. Additionally, you may want to experiment with different values for the "translateZ" and "rotateY" properties to achieve the desired effect. Keep in mind that excessive use of 3D effects can sometimes detract from the overall design, so it's important to use them judiciously.

