 To configure Mosquito MQTT broker optimally for accepting connections from Internet of Things (IoT) devices, consider the following settings in your `mosquito.conf` file:

1. Enable persistent client sessions to ensure that IoT devices can maintain their state even if they disconnect unexpectedly. Set this option under the `connection` section.

```
connection persistent_clients {
    type persistent;
};
```

2. Configure the maximum number of concurrent connections to manage the load on the MQTT broker. Set an appropriate value based on your hardware and network capacity.

```
listener 127.0.0.1 1883 {
   max_connections 500;
};

listener 0.0.0.0 1883 {
   protocol mqttv311;
   max_connections 500;
};
```

3. Allow anonymous access for simplicity with IoT devices, but be aware of the security implications. Set this option under the `access control` section.

```
acl anonymous_access allow guest;
```

4. Enable TLS (Transport Layer Security) encryption to secure communication between IoT devices and the MQTT broker. Generate an SSL certificate and key, then set these paths under the `listener` sections.

```
listener 0.0.0.0 8883 {
    protocol mqtts;
    cafile /path/to/your/certs/ca.crt;
    certfile /path/to/your/certs/server.crt;
    keyfile /path/to/your/certs/server.key;
};
```

5. Configure the `autosave_interval` and `autosave_time` options to enable automatic saving of messages in case of unexpected shutdown or restart of the MQTT broker.

```
persistence_file mosquito.db;
persistence_location /var/lib/mosquito/
persistence_size 1000000
autosave_interval 60
autosave_time 5
```

