 To configure a Chrome extension for injecting JavaScript into an existing webpage, you'll need to use a content script. Here's a step-by-step guide to help you get started:

1. Create a new directory for your Chrome extension and add the following boilerplate files:
   - `manifest.json` (at the root of the project)
   - `background.js` (in a subfolder named "bg")
   - `content.js` (in a subfolder named "content")

2. Configure the `manifest.json` file:

```json
{
  "manifest_version": 2,
  "name": "My Extension",
  "description": "A sample Chrome extension",
  "version": "1.0.0",
  "background": {
    "script": "bg/background.js"
  },
  "content_scripts": [
    {
      "matches": ["<http://*/*, <https://*/*>"],
      "js": ["content/content.js"]
    }
  ],
  "permissions": ["activeTab"]
}
```

In the above example, replace `"My Extension"` with the name of your extension and update the description as needed. The `content_scripts` section tells Chrome to inject `content.js` into all pages that match the specified URL pattern.

3. Write the JavaScript code in `content.js`:

```javascript
// Inject your custom JavaScript code here.
console.log('Content Script Injected!');
document.body.innerHTML += '<div id="myDiv">Hello, World!</div>';
```

Replace the `console.log()` statement with your own custom JavaScript code to modify the target webpage.

4. Initialize the background script in `background.js`:

```javascript
// background.js

chrome.runtime.onMessage.addListener(function (request, sender, sendResponse) {
  console.log('Received message from content script:', request);
});
```

The background script is initialized and listens for messages from the content script using the `chrome.runtime.onMessage` event.

